    "text_delta": "letters, reference checks, and compensation before shifting focus to unsuccessful candidates.\n* Formal written feedback often requires approval from both the panel chair and HR to ensure it aligns with official interview scorecards.\n* Delays are almost always bureaucratic rather than evasive.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up professionally:** Send a polite, concise follow-up email framed around professional development rather than grievance:\n   > *\"Thank you again for the opportunity to interview for the Head of Clinical Laboratory position. I understand the team is busy, but as I look to continue my professional growth, any brief written feedback from the panel regarding areas I could strengthen for future leadership opportunities would be greatly appreciated.\"*\n2. **Review the interview score criteria:** When you do receive feedback, look closely at where points were lost (e.g., strategic vision, financial management, governance) rather than the outcome itself.\n3. **Focus on operational leadership:** If you aim to step up from a section head to a department head, consider pursuing formal operational leadership certifications or hospital-wide committee roles to complement your scientific expertise.\n",
